# A committed TEMPLATE of .github/c2-serving-job.env for the tp4/next-2 window (scripts/ci/c2_serving_job.py parses it): copy it over .github/c2-serving-job.env on a throwaway commit of tp4/next-2 and push a tag
# experiment/c2-serving-vN. Nothing here names a rig, a card, a host or a registry: the cards are resolved at run time. The jobs are templates only: no tag is pushed from the branch that holds them.
# ONE IMAGE: tp4-next2-1 (B0's image; the drafter pack's D-T5 re-pointed from tp4-drafter-1). BEFORE PUSHING: run D-T1-control on tp4-next2-1 first, then replace the placeholder @CONTROL_RUN@ below with
# the digits of D-T1's workflow run id (sed -i 's/@CONTROL_RUN@/<digits>/' D-T5-lookup.env). The job reader REFUSES this template while the value is not digits.
# D-T5: the prompt-lookup tau arm lookup: the served drafter with QWEN_FAST_LOOKUP_DRAFT=n3m12 (docs/tp4-lookup.md: per user and per round a host-side lookup over that user's own token history
# replaces DFlash2's proposal rows when the last 3 tokens match earlier text for at least 12 tokens; the target verifies every row, so the text is the control's and only the accepted length moves)
# on the control image tp4-next2-1: no new image, nothing on a card changes. No A3. C2_TAULAB_PAIR_CONTROL below is the run id of the same-image D-T1 (digits; the job reader refuses a template without it).
# Nothing at startup shows the lookup (its engaged line is logged once per request), so the lab holds the arm to its markers in the CANARY after the first turns: the log must hold '[LOOKUP-DRAFT] engaged policy=n3m12'
# and at least one '[LOOKUP-ROUND]' line, or the lab stops (the lookup never engaged: every later turn would measure the control under this arm's name). The control arm shows neither line.
# The tau profile is c2-packed-tp4: it builds an engine per request. A profile that runs engine reuse (QWEN_FAST_PARKED_ENGINES=1, the production profile since the engine-reuse ship) cannot carry this arm: a rebound engine
# attaches no lookup and the contract refuses the pair, and the lab refuses such a base before it starts a container.
# Thinking ON, coding-agent turns from the public SWE-rebench set (A1) and the scrubbed own-session set (A2) as the lab already uses, no customer traffic; the data directory is rig-local and
# read-only to the job (kwork64/taulab/data under the runner's home), checked against its manifest before the container starts. The serving profile is c2-packed-tp4 with the lab's two
# arithmetic-neutral log flags and, for a drafter arm, only that arm's drafter-only flag. Judged PAIRED per turn id by drafter_pair_report.py against the control arm (the GO rule is in its
# docstring and docs/drafter-arms.md): pooled ratio lower 95%% bound > 1.00, no regression in the 80k+ bucket or at p10, identical text, both runs launched clean and asked the same questions, equal image tags; the control A3 verdict is an annotation.
# This is the first measurement of the lookup on real coding text with thinking ON through the tau lab. A GO is a tau finding for the owner, nothing more: what it costs a round (the index build at admission, the host time of a proposal) is judged
# in the timing pack (tp4-lookup-jobs, TL1-TL4), and serving it needs the lookup to run beside engine reuse first.
# Duration (estimate): 85 min (the deadline of 80 min is counted from the container start, the load included). Kill rule: the canary finding no lookup lines, a stall or a tripped breaker ends the arm.
C2_CARDS=quad
C2_ACTIONS=reset taulab
C2_IMAGE_TAG=tp4-next2-1
C2_TAULAB_PROFILE=c2-packed-tp4
C2_TAULAB_DRAFTER_ARM=lookup
C2_TAULAB_ARMS=A1 A2
C2_TAULAB_DEADLINE=80
C2_TAULAB_IN_FLIGHT=8
C2_TAULAB_PAIR_CONTROL=@CONTROL_RUN@
